In this work, for the first time, a new magnetic cadmium-based MOFs $(\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F})$ was successfully syn­ thesized in a green way and then modified with chitosan (CS) in the microsphere form $(\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS})$ . The obtained materials were fully characterized by several techniques. In the following, the efficiency of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ was explored for the removal of amoxicillin (AMX). The outcome of the adsorption study showed that the removal efficiency is affected by CS and reaches its optimum at $\mathtt{p H}8$ and contact time of 240 min. Under optimized conditions, over $75\%$ of AMX was removed. The kinetic and the isotherm of the adsorption were fit with the pseudo-second-order model and the Langmuir adsorption isotherm respectively. Eventually, the maximum adsorption capacity was obtained ${\sim}103.09\mathrm{mg/g}$ . Interestingly, these findings convince that the newly prepared $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ could be proposed as a promising magnetically separable adsorbent for antibiotic contaminants removal from the aqueous solution.
## 1. Introduction
Water is the more vital substance on earth, so any pollution of it causes harmful effects for the whole of living creatures [1–4]. In recent decades the water pollutions have become an alarming risk to the ecosystem, human, and animals. The release of the pollutants such as heavy metals, herbicides, pesticides, and pharmaceuticals into the water leads to its pollution and reduces its quality [5–7]. Today's pharma­ ceutical residuals are suggested as an overgrowing portion of the emerging pollutants because of their broad usage [8]. Antibiotics, in particular, AMX is the most usually used oral bactericidal antibiotic which belongs to the $\upbeta$ -lactam family. The structure of AMX has amphoteric features because of the presence of the three main functional groups; –COOH (pKa 2.69), –OH (pKa 9.63), and $\mathrm{-NH}_{2}$ (pKa 7.49). It is shown that most pharmaceutical products have resistant to degrada­ tion, hence, their introduction into the environment leads to serious problems even in low concentrations [9,10]. For this reason, the development of operative treatment procedures for the removal of an­ tibiotics is necessary [8]. Numerous treatment approaches have been developed for antibiotics removal which the biodegradation, photo­ catalytic adsorption, membrane filtration, advanced oxidation proced­ ures (AOPs), and ozonation are some examples [11–14]. Comparatively, the adsorption process has an extensive advantage and it is considered the most powerful treatment way in terms of its economic sustainability, convenience, recyclability, ease process, high efficiency, short time, etc. [5]. Despite these advantages, this method suffers from the difficulty of the adsorbent separating from the aqueous media [9]. Therefore, nowadays scientists have been developed numerous many magnetically nano-based adsorbents for overcoming this limitation [15–17]. The combination of the nanomaterials; typically the combination of magnetite nanoparticles (NPs) with porous materials in for example MOFs improves their properties and also obtain a new system that has the advantage of both constituents [18–24]. MOFs as a new class of nanoporous materials commonly display high efficiency in the adsorp­ tion process [7,25–27]. Moreover, they can provide effective protection that maintains the stability of the magnetic NPs [1]. The high adsorption efficiency of MOFs are generally related to their large specific surface area, metal ion doping, available pores, versatile framework structures, intriguing functionalities, hydrogen-bonding, $\pi{-}\pi$ interactions among the aromatic backbone of the pollutant and the aromatic rings of MOFs, as well as the electrostatic interactions [5,28,29].
The composition of the nanohybrids with polymers can bring a new advantage to the nanohybrid-based adsorbent [30–32]. Therefore, to­ day's significant research has been paid consideration to the polymeric nanocomposite-based adsorbents to gather the synergistic effect of both components; nanohybrids and also polymers. However, the use of syn­ thetic polymers is associated with several unwanted problems like lack of biodegradability. So the dispersion of residual polymers or monomers in water may lead to a health hazard. By considering this the researchers try to fabricate the adsorbents based on natural eco-friendly polymers. Protein, cellulose, starch, and CS are some commonly used polymers for the synthesis of natural polymer-based adsorbents. Among the natural polymers, a great deal of attention has been devoted to CS [33–36]. This non-toxic, biodegradable, low cost and commercially available biopolymer CS is the second most abundant natural biopolymer after cellulose [37,38]. Additionally, the other advantage of the CS used for contaminants removal is related to the presence of oxygen or nitrogencontaining functional groups which can provide abundant active sites for the adsorption of pollutants, as well as, it can improve the wettability [39,40]. Taking the abovementioned into account in this work is aimed to design, prepare, and evaluate the efficacy of a new environmentally friendly CS-based adsorbent; $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ for removal of the AMX from aqueous solutions. To the best of our knowledge, this work is the first report for exploring $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ as an adsorbent of AMX. Indeed, the main goal of the present work was to introduce the more biodegradable and biocompatible CS as a green coat on the adsorbent structure and evaluate its effect on the adsorption process. Therefore, to achieve such a goal, in this work, a new magnetically separable ternary was prepared via coating of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathrm{Cd}}$ -MOF with CS biopolymer in the microsphere shape. Then the developed structures were fully characterized. Different factors; pH value, initial concentra­ tion of drug, contact time, adsorbent dosage were optimized in thee AMX adsorption tests. Moreover, the kinetics and isotherm models were employed to describe respectively the mechanism and equilibrium isotherms.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Chemicals and reagents
CS (medium molecular weight; MW 50,000–190,000 Da and deacetylation degree of $80.3\%)$ , ferric chloride hexahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ , ferrous chloride tetrahydrate $(\mathrm{FeCl}_{4}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ , and ammonia (25 $\mathrm{wt}\%\dot{}$ ) were supplied from Sigma-Aldrich. Dimethylformamide (DMF), terephthalic acid, cadmium acetate dihydrate (Cd $(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COO})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ , glacial acetic acid, Tween 80, Span 80, magnesium stearate, acetone, petroleum ether, isopropyl alcohol, sodium hydroxide (NaOH), and hydrochloric acid (HCl) reagents were purchased from Merck. AMX with a purity of $98\%$ was provided by Danna Pharma Co. (Tabriz, Iran). Bidistilled water was employed in overall this work.
