## 2.4. $F e_{3}O_{4}@C d$ -MOF nanohybrid synthesis
For the preparation of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs (0.5199 g) were well dispersed in $14.96\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF for $30\mathrm{min}$ to obtain the stable $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs. In the following, terephthalic acid $(2.7279\ \mathrm{mmol}$ , $\phantom{-}0.453\ g)$ was poured into the reaction mixture and the mixture was agitated for 30 min. Subsequently, in the ultrasonic cleaner, the solution of Cd $(\mathrm{CH_{3}C O O})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H_{2}O}$ $(0.0711\ \mathrm{mol}$ , $\ensuremath{1.28~\mathrm{g})}$ was added drop by drop to the mixture of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}.$ /terephthalic acid for $30\mathrm{min}$ . The ultra-sonication was followed for $30~\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. The product was collected from the mixture by an external magnetic force and washed with DMF $(2\times20~\mathrm{mL})$ and then with water $(2\times20~\mathrm{mL})$ . Eventually, the obtained $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF nanohybrid was dried in the vacuum oven at $80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The neat Cd-MOF was fabricated in the same way described above without the addition of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs.
## 2.5. Fabrication of Fe3O4@Cd-MOF@CS microspheres
The medium molecular weight of CS solution was prepared by dis­ solving the CS $_{(0.6g)}$ in an aqueous acetic acid solution $20\mathrm{mL}$ , $2\%\mathrm{v/v})$ under agitation at room temperature. Afterward, the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}$ $_{(0.12g)}$ was poured gradually into the CS solution. The mixture was well homogenized employing an ultrasonic bath for $30~\mathrm{min}$ . Subsequently, the mixture was dropwise added into the oil medium ( $60~\mathrm{mL}$ of olive oil containing $0.3\mathrm{g}$ of Tween 80, $0.9\mathrm{g}$ of Span 80, and $_{0.1\ g}$ of magnesium stearate). The mixture was heated at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ The solution of vanillin in acetone $(10\%$ , v/v) was added dropwise into the suspension and then the formed microspheres were stirred for another $^{3\mathrm{h}}$ . In the following, the formed $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres were separated from the suspension via centrifuging and fully washed with petroleum ether fol­ lowed by isopropyl alcohol. The produced $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ micro­ spheres were dried in a vacuum for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Pristin CS microspheres were prepared also by a similar procedure mentioned above without the addition of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF [42].
2.6. Point of zero charge (pHpzc) determination for $F e_{3}O_{4}@C d.$ MOF@CS microspheres
$\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{PZC}}$ of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres was determined via recording the changes in the initial pH of the aqueous solution as a result of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres addiction. Briefly, at first, the aqueous solutions in the $\mathsf{p H}$ range of 3–10 were prepared via pH adjusting with $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{NaOH}}$ or HCl. In the next step, the sample was poured into each solution. The obtained mixtures were shaken for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The final pH of the solutions was determined and denoted as $\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{f}}$ . Then the curve of $\mathsf{p H}_{\mathrm{i}^{-}\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{f}}}$ ( pH) was plotted versus $\mathrm{\pH_{i}}$ . Finally, the pHPZC of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres was specified from the point of intersection of the curve at which $\Delta\mathrm{pH}=0$ .
## 2.7. AMX adsorption studies
The bath adsorption of MTX on the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ micro­ spheres was studied in a conical glass vessel. In the adsorption studies the $\mathsf{p H}$ of the adsorption solutions was adjusted by using the dilute NaOH or HCl solution; $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ . Typically, $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ micro­ spheres $\mathrm{(50~mg)}$ were immersed in $100\mathrm{mL}$ of AMX with a concentration of $50~\mathrm{ppm}$ . The vessel was sealed and placed on a shaker with a rate of $150~\mathrm{rpm}$ at ambient temperature. After each selected time, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@{\cal C}\mathrm{d}-$ $\operatorname{MOF@CS}$ microspheres were separated from the suspensions and the concentration of the remained AMX in the adsorption solution was measured by using UV–Vis spectroscopy at $\lambda=273\mathrm{nm}$ . After that, Eqs. (1) and (2) were used to measure the adsorption capacity and removal percentage. All experimental measurements were performed in tripli­ cate and their averaged were reported.
