where ${\bf C}_{0}$ (mg/L), ${\sf C}_{\mathrm{e}}$ (mg/L), V (L), and $\mathfrak{m}\left(\mathfrak{g}\right)$ respectively indicates the concentrations of AMX solution at the initial time, concentrations of AMX solution at equilibrium time, the volume of the AMX solution, and the weight of the used $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres for doing each of batch adsorption experiment.
After determinating the equilibrium time, a series of experiments were performed to obtain the optimum pH (3–10), adsorbent dose $\left(30{-}90~\mathrm{mg}\right)$ , and initial concentration of AMX $\mathrm{25-200~mg/L)}$ in the selected time. The found optimized value of each parameter was utilized in the subsequent experiments for kinetic and isotherm studies.
## 2.8. Desorption and regeneration experiments
The reusability of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres was evaluated with $40\%$ methanol solution. Briefly, after AMX adsorption, the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres were separated from the liquid phase and washed thoroughly with $40\%$ methanol solution then it was dried in a vacuum oven. Subsequently, the adsorbent was reused for the subsequent adsorption test. This operation; adsorption-desorption was repeated to obtain the number of cycles that $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres could be used in acceptable efficiency.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Preparation of Fe3O4@Cd-MOF@CS microspheres
In the development of the new adsorbents for water treatment, in the current work, for the first time, a new magnetic and porous polysaccharide-based adsorbent was prepared via facile coating of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}$ with CS and afterward was evaluated for AMX removal. In typical procedure and by considering the advantages of ultrasoundassisted synthesis like short reaction time, synthesis at room tempera­ ture and ambient pressure, and high quality of the final products in the current work at first the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs were directly prepared under ul­ trasound. In the following, the ultrasound-assisted synthesis of Cd-MOF in the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs obtained a magnetic and porous nano­ hybrid. Eventually, the $\mathbf{Fe}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF was capsulated with CS in the water-in-oil process to improve adsorption capacity and also safety. Fig. 1 represents the fabrication route of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ micro­ spheres. The presence of the magnetite NPs can induce magnetic sepa­ ration which is a good technique in the filtration step. On the other hand, the MOFs have the advantages like exceptional porosity, high surface area, and easy functionalization which able them to show good removal ability toward many different pollutants. Finally, CS as a biodegradable polymer has become a hot point in the adsorption area, particularly from the environmental concern and cost viewpoints. In addition, CS is one of the importantly studied natural polymers owing to the presence of active hydroxyl and amino functional groups in its structure [30,43]. Consid­ ering these, the combination of magnetic MOFs nanohybrid with CS can obtain a safe and easily separable system with improved adsorption capacity. Finally after the detailed characterization, the efficiency of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres was studied as an AMX adsorbent.
## 3.2. Characterization of Fe3O4@Cd-MOF@CS microspheres
## 3.2.1. FT-IR analysis
FT-IR spectroscopy was accomplished to investigate the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}$ nanohybrid and its coating with CS through the analysis of the chemical functional groups according to their FT-IR spectra (Fig. 2a). For pristine $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs, the appeared significant absorption band at $588~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ is related to the stretching vibration of Fe–O. Moreover, two detected characteristic peaks at $1636~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $3396\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are related to the hydroxyl groups that covered their surface during the synthesis via the chemical co-precipitation method. The FTIR spectrum of synthesized neat Cd-MOF showed typical vibrational band peaks at $3444\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $3121\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $3055\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and $1579\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ which could be ascribed by $\scriptstyle0-\mathrm{H}$ stretching vibration, ${\mathsf{C}}{\mathrm{-}}{\mathrm{H}}$ stretching vibra­ tions belong the ring, and stretching vibration of $\mathsf{C}{\mathop{=}}\mathsf{C}$ . In this FT-IR spectrum, two located peaks at $1506~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $1368~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ are assigned to the asymmetric and symmetric stretching vibrations of $-\mathtt{C O O}^{-}$ groups of Cd-MOF, signifying the successful synthesis of CdMOFs via coordination of Cd atoms. Compared with Cd-MOF, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF exhibited a peak at $546~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ with an enhanced in­ tensity that related to stretching vibration of Fe–O and demonstrates the successful growth of Cd-MOF in the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs. In the case of CS the stretching vibration of the $-\mathrm{CH}$ , $\scriptstyle{\mathsf{C}}=0$ of acetamide, bending vibration of the $\mathrm{{N-H}}$ , stretching vibration of the C–N, and stretching vibration of the $\mathtt{C-O}$ observed at $2875~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1639~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $1606~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ , $1295{-}1427~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , and $1000{-}1290~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . In this spectrum, the hydroxyl, and amine groups, as well as, intermolecular and intra­ molecular hydrogen bonds were detected at $3600{-}3400\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The FT-IR spectrum of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres illuminates the distinc­ tive peaks that belong to the components of the microspheres. In this spectrum, the new appeared major characteristic peak at $1288~\mathrm{{cm}}^{-1}$ belongs to $-\mathrm{OH}$ of vanillin. Additionally, the peak at $1647~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ arose from the stretching vibration of the formed $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathrm{N}$ because of the Schiff's base reaction between the amino group of CS with the aldehyde group of vanillin; corresponding to the CS crosslinking with vanillin. Overall the achieved FT-IR outcome could be good evidence for the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}$ d-MOF and asserted its coating with CS.
## 3.2.2. XRD analysis
The crystallinity of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs, $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}@{\mathrm{Cd}}$ -MOF nanohybrid, and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres was tested by XRD analysis (Fig. 2b). The XRD pattern of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs showed the main peaks at $2\theta30.3^{\circ}$ , $35.9^{\circ}$ , $43.4^{\circ}$ , $53.7^{\circ}$ , $57.2^{\circ}$ , and $62.8^{\circ}$ which respectively corresponds to the crystalline planes of (220), (311), (400), (422), (511), and (440). The observed XRD spectrum is in good accordance with the reported standard pattern (JCPDS card No. 19-0629) for $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\ \mathrm{NPs}$ with a facecentered cubic (fcc) structure. The average crystal size was calculated by Scherrer equation about $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ ; considering the full width at half maximum of the peak at $35.9^{\circ}$ . In addition to these peaks, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF manifested several new peaks located at $26\ 12.3^{\circ}$ , $14.8^{\circ}$ , $17.8^{\circ}$ , $25.4^{\circ}$ , $28.0^{\circ}$ , $32.7^{\circ}$ , $36.1^{\circ}$ , $42.3^{\circ}$ , $50.9^{\circ}$ , $59.6^{\circ}$ , $65.1^{\circ}$ and $67.1^{\circ}$ of its XRD pattern which is mainly ascribed to the Cd-MOF specie. The presence of the wide peaks approves the nano-structural nature of both compounds [44,45]. Based on the obtained XRD spectrum it can be concluded that the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}$ was successfully prepared and it is composed of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs and Cd-MOF. After coating of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF with CS biopolymer, a broad peak appeared at $2615^{\circ}-24^{\circ}$ that confirms the amorph nature of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres due to cross­ linking. Moreover, the XRD spectrum of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ mi­ crospheres showed some of the main peaks of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}$ with a slight intensity that results from the successful coating of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@{\cal C}\mathrm{d}\cdot$ MOF in the amorph network of CS. Overall the XRD results imply the existence of an amorphous CS shell around the crystalline $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@{\mathsf{C d}}\cdot$ MOF and could be strong evidence for the successful fabrication of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres.
## 3.2.3. SEM and EDX analysis
The surface morphology and also the structure of the fabricated samples were explored by SEM and the obtained images are shown in Fig. 3. As apparent from the photograph, the synthesized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs presented a smooth and monodispersed spherical structure with an average size of about $20\mathrm{nm}$ . Also, this image shows that the synthesized $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs generally are uniformly shaped. The obtained surface morphology for the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}$ is different from the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs. Therefore, as seen, the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs were attached to the surface of CdMOF crystals. In other words, fabricated chalk-like Cd-MOF in the presence of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs showed good contact with each other. Good contact between $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs and Cd-MOF can improve the efficiency of the prepared system as an adsorbent from the porosity and magnetic property viewpoints. SEM image demonstrated a spherical morphology with a rougher outer surface and diameter of $15{-}25~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ for $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@{\mathsf{C d}}\cdot$ $\operatorname{MOF@CS}$ microspheres. The rough surface of the microspheres permits improved AMX adsorption and leads to a higher activity. In the following, the microspheres were evaluated in other magnification. The distributed dots on the obtained image demonstrated the dispersion of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF in the polymer matrix. Overall, the SEM analysis results confirmed the synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF and its good distribution in the CS microspheres.
For further evaluation of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres synthesis and the dispersion quality of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}$ , the chemical composition of the elements and their distribution in the structure of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres respectively were assessed via EDX spectrum and the element mapping. The obtained spectrum and element maps are depicted in Fig. 4a and b, respectively. The achieved EDX spectrum specified only the presence of C, O, N, Cd, and Fe elements respectively with a percentage of $49.69\%$ , $32.95\%$ , $13.26\%$ , $2.17\%$ , and $1.94\%$ in $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres structure. Also, EDX mapping showed that all of the elements homogenously are distributed in the structure. This uniform distribution of the Cd and Fe as a main element of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF approves its homogenous distribution in the polymer network which can be caused to the more efficiency of each adsorbent.
## 3.2.4. VSM and BET analysis
The magnetic property of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d\cdot M O F}$ , and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@{\mathsf{C d}}\cdot$ $\operatorname{MOF@CS}$ microspheres was measured by using a vibrating sample magnetometer at ambient temperature. Fig. 5a displays the obtained VSM spectra of the studied samples. Evaluation of the achieved VSM curves exhibited that all of these three samples are superparamagnetic. The saturation magnetization of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF, and $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres, respectively were acquired to be $61.35\mathrm{\emug^{-1}}$ , $27.7\mathrm{\emug^{-1}}$ , and $3.6\mathrm{emu}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The observed decreasing trend in the magnetization strength can be attributed to the introduction of the non-magnetic Cd-MOF and CS materials in the structure of the samples which depresses the magnetic value of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}$ NPs. Despite this, the measured saturation magnetization is favorable and approves the suitable magnetism nature of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres in reasoning to an external magnet. All results from VSM are in good accordance with XRD output and provided qualitative evidence that the synthesis of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres has occurred successfully.
The porosity of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres structure was also explored through the ${\bf N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption experiment (Fig. 5b and c). A study of the obtained isotherm in IUPAC classification signi­ fying the porosity of the system. After doing this analysis the surface area, pore-volume, and mean pore diameter of the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF $@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres respectively were found about $4.4445\mathrm{m}^{2}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , $0.0065\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ ${\bf g}^{-1}$ , and $5.8677{\mathrm{nm}}$ . By considering the smaller size of AMX $(\sim21.2\mathring{\mathrm{A}})$ the mesopores structure of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres could allow AMX to penetration into the porous sites, which is convenient for adsorbent and develops its adsorption performance.
## 3.2.5. Determination of pHpzc
pH is one of the main factors which play a key role in the adsorption procedure because it determines the ionization degree of contaminant molecules and the surface charge of adsorbents. For the solution with a pH of higher than pHpzc, the surface functional groups of the adsorbent will be deprotonated. However, for the solution with a lower pH value than pHpzc, the surface is positive owing to the protonation of the present functional groups. By considering these, the point of zero elec­ tric charge pH was determined for the designed adsorbent in this work; $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres. As it is depicted in Fig. 5d, the pHpzc was obtained ${\sim}8.1$ , signifying $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ micro­ spheres has positively charged at a lower pH than 8.6. This pHpzc dis­ plays the favoring of negatively charged species adsorption on $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres via electrostatic interaction.
## 3.3. Adsorption studies
## 3.3.1. Effect of contact time
The adsorption experiments were carried out for AMX removal as a model of the frequently used antibiotic drugs in human infection ther­ apy. In this regard, at the beginning of the adsorption study, the timedependent removal of AMX by $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres was evaluated to obtain the time that equilibrium reaches. Fig. 6a displays the time-dependent removal of AMX from the aqueous solution under ambient conditions. Accuracy on the obtained adsorption diagram re­ veals that the adsorption process consists of three steps. In the first step, the adsorption of AMX was increased rapidly, so, after $50\ \mathrm{min}$ the adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres reached $75.40\ \mathrm{mg/g}$ . The availability of more active adsorption sites, as well as the more gradient in concentration of AMX between the solution and adsorbent, could be the reasons for the fast adsorption of AMX in the initial times. In the following owing to reduction of the active adsorption sites availability, as well as a lower gradient in concentration of AMX, the AMX adsorption rate becomes slower. Finally, the equilibrium of adsorption reached after $240~\mathrm{min}$ . Therefore, $240~\mathrm{{min}}$ was selected as the optimal time for the adsorption process. These findings exhibit that the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres can remove the acceptable amount of amoxicillin from wastewater.
## 3.3.2. Effect of solution $p H$
The pH of the solution has the main influence on the adsorption of the pollutants on the adsorbent surface because it can affect the charge of the organic compound and also the surface charge of the adsorbent. Therefore, the effect of the AMX solution pH was investigated in ranging from 3 to 10 to find the optimum pH that leads to the maximum adsorption. As it is reported in Fig. 6b, the percentage of the AMX removal increased from $22.07\%$ for $\mathrm{pH}3$ to $73.92\%$ for $\mathtt{p H}8$ , but, it was decreased with a more increase in the pH of the solution. The obtained trend can be explained based on both the surface charge of the adsorbent and the AMX dissociation constant (pKa); AMX has the pKa of 2.4, 7.4, and 9.6 respectively for carboxyl, amine, and phenol groups. Consid­ ering the obtained pHpzc for $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres, in lower $\mathsf{p H}$ value, the sample has a positive surface charge, but, the car­ boxylic acid functional groups (-COOH) of AMX are in carboxylate $(-\mathrm{coo^{-}})$ ) form. As a result, electrostatic attraction between the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres and AMX could be a predominant proof for the observed trend. The decrease in the AMX adsorption at pH ${\tt>p H p z c}$ could be associated with electrostatic repulsion between AMX and negative charge on the surface of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ micro­ spheres, as well as the competition between more hydroxyl anions in water with pharmaceutical anions. However, it could be detected that the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres still adsorb AMX when the pH is higher than 8.1 which electrostatic repulsion is predominant. This specifies that the other factors drive AMX adsorption. π-π stacking in­ teractions between the aromatic rings in AMX and benzene rings in CdMOF and hydrogen bond interactions between AMX and $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@{\cal C}\mathrm{d}.$ -
MOF nanohybrid or CS are two other possible adsorption driving forces in this condition. Overall, results indicate the adsorption potential of prepared $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres for the maximum adsorp­ tion of AMX in a solution with a pH of 6 which was chosen as the optimal pH for further experiments.
## 3.3.3. Effect of adsorbent dosage
The adsorbent dosage is effected the adsorption procedure since it can influence the neat surface charge amount and also the total surface area. As can be seen in Fig. 6c, the removal of AMX increased with increasing the adsorbent dosage from $30~\mathrm{mg}$ to $50\mathrm{mg}$ . This observation can be related to an increase in the neat charge and total specific surface area. Afterward, the adsorption becomes nearly constant which may be related to the establishment of equilibrium between the AMX molecule on the adsorbent and in the solution. On the other hand, the observed opposite trend for the equilibrium adsorption capacity may be related to aggregation or overlapping of adsorption sites that lead to the decrease in total adsorption surface area. Therefore, $50~\mathrm{mg}$ was chosen as the optimum adsorbent dosage for the removal of AMX using the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres.
## 3.3.4. Effect of initial concentration of AMX
The effect of AMX concentrations (25, 50, 100, 150, and $200~\mathrm{{mg/L})}$ was explored on its adsorption capacity of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF@CS~n~}$ i­ crospheres under the obtained optimum conditions (Fig. 6d). It was observed that an increase in the initial concentration of AMX to $200\mathrm{mg/}$ mL increases the adsorption capacity of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ mi­ crospheres to $97.63~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . Indeed with an increase in AMX concentra­ tion, the gradient between the aqueous phase and the adsorbent enhances which can act as a driving force for this trend. These findings exhibit the great tendency of the prepared $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ mi­ crospheres for adsorption of the AMX molecules; maximum adsorption achieved for $200~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ of initial concentration.
## 3.3.5. Kinetics of AMX adsorption
Adsorption kinetic models could obtain valuable information about the adsorption process rate, as well as the adsorption pathways. Therefore the pseudo-first-order (Eq. (3)) and pseudo-second-order (Eq. (4)) kinetic models as the most commonly used models were investi­ gated to assess the adsorption mechanism of AMX onto $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@{\cal C}\mathrm{d}\cdot$ $\operatorname{MOF@CS}$ microspheres. In this context, the kinetic studies were per­ formed by measuring the AMX concentration at certain time intervals in optimum conditions. Finally, the obtained correlation coefficients $(\mathsf{R}^{2})$ were used to determine the accordance of experimental results with each of them.
$$
\frac{t}{q_{t}}=\frac{1}{k_{2}q_{e}^{2}}+\frac{1}{q_{e}}t
$$
Herein the $\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\ (\mathbf{m}\mathbf{g}/\mathbf{g})$ and $\mathsf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}$ $\left(\mathrm{mg}/\mathrm{g}\right)$ are the amounts of adsorbed AMX at equilibrium and time t (min) respectively. Moreover, the $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ (1/ min) and $\mathbf{k}_{2}$ $\mathrm{:(g/(mg\min)})$ are the rates constant.
Therefore, to understand how the adsorption of AMX onto $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres, the pseudo-first-order, and pseudosecond-order kinetic models were utilized to fit the achieved experi­ mental data. The obtained results are reported in Table 1, Fig. 7a, and b. The comparison of the measured correlation coefficients $(\mathbb{R}^{2})$ from experimental data suggests that the adsorption is well fitted to a pseudosecond-order model and this model with the higher values of $\scriptstyle\mathrm{\mathsf{R}}^{2}$ can be used to describe the kinetics of the adsorption process. These results indicated that the physio-chemical process involved during the adsorption.
## 3.3.6. Isotherm of AMX adsorption
The adsorption isotherms specify the how the distribution of adsor­ bate between the solid and liquid phases. Thus, the two most common equations; Langmuir (Eq. (5)) and Freundlich (Eq. (6)) isotherm models were employed to evaluate the adsorption equilibrium under the optimal condition with various initial drug concentrations (25, 50, 100, 150, and $200\mathrm{mg/mL}$ ). Then the obtained experimental data were fitted with their linear equations.
$$
\begin{array}{l}{\displaystyle{\frac{\mathbb{C}_{e}}{q_{e}}=\frac{1}{q_{0}K_{L}}+\frac{C_{e}}{q_{0}}}}\\ {\displaystyle{\mathrm{Ln~q}_{e}=L n K_{F}+\frac{1}{n}L n C_{e}}}\end{array}
$$
where $\mathrm{q_{e}(m g/g),C_{e}(m g/L)_{:}}$ , and ${\bf q}_{0}\left({\bf m g/g}\right)$ are the amount of adsorbed AMX at equilibrium time, the equilibrium concentration of AMX, and maximum adsorption capacity respectively. While the $\mathrm{K_{L}}$ $_\mathrm{L}(\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{mg})$ and $\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{f}}$ $((\mathbf{mg/g})(\mathbf{L/mg})^{1/n})$ respectively are the Langmuir and Freundlich constant.
Therefore, in this work the experimental equilibrium adsorption data were assessed with two common isotherm models; Langmuir and Freundlich to determine the maximum sorption capacity (Fig. 7c, d, and Table 2). The found $\mathrm{\ttR}^{2}$ value for the Langmuir isotherm was greater than Freundlich isotherm. The fitting with Langmuir isotherm shows that the AMX adsorption on $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres is in high accordance with the Langmuir model and adsorption occurred as a monolayer on homogenously adsorption sites on the surfaces of the adsorbents. The maximum monolayer adsorption capacity of AMX on $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres was calculated at $103.09\mathrm{\mg/g}$ . Overall the finding specifies that the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres could be a promising candidate for application as an adsorbent to manage water pollution.
In the following, the obtained results of the current study were compared with the reported results in the previously published litera­ ture for AMX adsorption via different nanocomposites. The outcomes are presented in Table 3. As it can be seen from Table 3, the fabricated $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres exhibited a higher and better maximum adsorption capacity than some of the other previously listed carriers, specifying that $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres are an appropriate adsorbent for AMX removal. The following could be pro­ posed as the advantage of our fabricated system. (I) The presence of the magnetite NPs provides the possibility of facile magnetic separation. (II) Compared with the previous CS-based adsorbent without MOFs, the porosity of the structure as well as the aromatic rings in the structure, remarkably improved removal efficiency, because of its additionally enhanced interactions with AMX. Finally, (III) coating with CS increases the biocompatibility and the number of the oxygen and amine functional groups which provide the AMX removal via electrostatic interaction and hydrogen bonding.
## 3.3.7. Possible adsorption mechanisms
$\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ is a new nanocomposite with several types of functional groups which can participate in the AMX adsorption. (I) CS contains numerous hydroxyl and amine functional groups which can adsorb MTX by electrostatic attraction and hydrogen bonding. (II) the $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@$ Cd-MOF nanohybrid contains abundant porous sites and aro­ matic rings which respectively provide the possibility for trapping of the contaminants inside cavities and $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking interaction between the aromatic ring of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}$ and AMX which can further promote adsorption [51–53]. The proposed adsorption mechanism for AMX adsorption is depicted in Fig. 8.
## 3.3.8. Effect of temperature
The effect of the AMX solution temperature was explored at four selected temperatures: $15^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ $35^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ and $45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . As it is presented in Fig. 9a, the adsorption capacity of AMX on the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres was increased with increasing the solution temperature from $15~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ but further increase in the solution temperature decreased it. The maximum adsorption was observed at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ Expan­ sion of the active sites and their attraction to the adsorbate may be the reason for the observed enhancement. While the further increase of temperature reduced this efficiency because of increased thermal movement of AMX molecules which declines the attraction force be­ tween the AMX and adsorbent.
## 3.3.9. Desorption studies and reusability of Fe3O4@Cd-MOF@CS microspheres
The reusability of each adsorbent is a vital criterion for its large-scale applications. Therefore the reusability of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ micro­ spheres was studied in the optimum condition for five consecutive cy­ cles. Typically after the adsorption of AMX, the adsorbent dispersed in $40\%$ methanol solution and then washed. In the following, the regen­ erated sample was used to absorb AMX. As seen in Fig. 9b, during the 5 adsorption-desorption cycles the removal percentage of AMX change from $73.92\%$ to $62.07\%$ . A similar decrease $(11.58\%)$ was observed in the previously reported adsorbents and the change may be due to the incomplete desorption of AMX caused by repeated adsorptiondesorption processes. Additionally, the loss of a small amount of adsorbent within the recycling could be another reason for the observed trend [54]. Overall these results showed an acceptable recycling capa­ bility of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres and induced it's as an economical and effective adsorbent which directed it as a potential candidate for water purification.
## 4. Conclusion
In this study, $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}}@\mathrm{Cd-MOF}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres were prepared through the facile coating of the ultrasound-assisted synthesized magnetic cadmium metal-organic frameworks via CS as a biocompatible polymer. After verifying the successful preparation of $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{d}}\cdot$ MOF $@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres via FT-IR, VSM, XRD, SEM, EDX, and BET an­ alyses its adsorption potential was examined in the adsorption of AMX. pHpzc of $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres was determined about 8.1. The fabricated adsorbent exhibited acceptable adsorption efficiency toward AMX. Sensitivity to the pH of the adsorption process confirms the important role of CS in it. The $\pi{-}\pi$ stacking, electrostatic interaction, and hydrogen bonding are the three main possible participated factors in the observed adsorption values. The maximum adsorption capacity of the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres was found ${\sim}103.09~\mathrm{mg/g}$ . Desorp­ tion studies showed that the $\mathrm{Fe_{3}O_{4}@C d-M O F}@\mathrm{CS}$ microspheres have a good recycling capability after 5 cycles. The adsorption kinetics and isotherm followed the pseudo-second-order and Langmuir model, respectively. Finally, the outcomes demonstrated that $\mathrm{Fe}_{3}{\cal O}_{4}@{\cal C}\mathrm{d}-$ $\operatorname{MOF@CS}$ microspheres have the potential to be proposed as a low-cost and efficient adsorbent for AMX removal from contaminated water.
